# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment template — copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in a value.
#
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is git-ignored (see .gitignore → `.env*.local`) so every
# developer / machine / network keeps its own value without ever editing
# source code. Restart `npx expo start` after changing it — Expo inlines
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` values at bundle time.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Stable backend base URL. Drives BOTH the REST client and the WebSocket
# tracking channel (ws/wss is derived from this single value), so this is
# the only URL you ever configure.
#
# RECOMMENDED — a Cloudflare *named* tunnel gives a fixed URL (under YOUR
# domain) that survives every Wi-Fi change (home / campus / hotspot), so
# `EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL` is set ONCE and never edited again. See
# scripts/setup-named-tunnel.md for the one-time setup (login + tunnel
# create + route dns). After that, restarting the laptop, switching
# networks, or rebooting Expo does not require touching this file.
#
# FALLBACK (not recommended) — `cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:8080`
# mints a fresh `*.trycloudflare.com` URL on every restart. The app keeps
# working, but you'll be editing this line after every cloudflared
# restart — exactly the "Network request failed" failure mode this
# template exists to avoid. Ngrok is the same pattern: `ngrok http 8080`
# → use its `https://` URL here.
#
# Leave this UNSET on an Android emulator / iOS simulator — the app falls
# back to probing the loopback aliases automatically.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://dev-api.your-domain.com
